Source and Target are roles, and DefinitionPath moves to the generator's carrier side

Dev's grounding, in substance: Drake is a Domain and is a Source — it parses to the normative form,
and no particular carrier (JSON today; XML, YAML, protobuf later) needs to be normative to the
framework, only faithful under the Drake <-> carrier round-trip. ScalaTarget is a Domain and is a
Target, and is the type parameter of Generator. Generator(ScalaTarget) is a domain whose members are
morphisms, as rules, from a definition to Scala source. And: seeing anything Scala- or Java-dependent
in drake is regressive against the language neutrality of the definition language.

None of this was grounded consistently in the corpus, and two things this commit does follow from it.

THE ROLES WERE CROSSED

`draco.Source` has existed since the first domain unification as an empty marker with exactly one
deriver: `ScalaTarget from draco Source`. Under the grounding that is the wrong role. `Drake` derived
no role. `draco.Target` did not exist. The one place the corpus said anything about Source and Target,
it said the opposite.

    draco.Target                   new — an empty role marker, the twin of Source, member of the root domain
    ScalaTarget from draco Target  (was: from draco Source)
    Drake       from draco Source  (was: no derivation)

Roles are held as marker types a domain derives, the way Holon and Primal already are — draco's
existing spelling of "is a", not a new mechanism. README's domain table carries the grounding, and
the reconciliation it gives: Ash_Birch's `source`/`target` keywords are PAIRWISE; Generator(L) is
target-parametric and SOURCE-UNIVERSAL — any Source yielding a TypeDefinition feeds it — which is why
it carries a type parameter and no `source` keyword, and why DefinitionPath belongs on the generator's
source side rather than inside Drake.

THE GENERATOR'S SOURCE SIDE, NAMED

`draco.generator.carrier`, domain type Carrier. A carrier is the format a definition travels in
between Drake and the loader; the sub-domain holds what is ABOUT carriers rather than about
definitions — where they are found (DefinitionPath, now), how their bytes are read and decoded
(SourceContent, TypeLoader — the same question, deferred; TypeLoader is the hard one, since every
generated companion calls it). It is CONTAINED under generator, not derived from Generator(L): it is
target-independent, so there is no L to instantiate, and containment is not inheritance — Dev's own
correction from the forest scenario. Carrier's domain aspect self-loops; its types list holds
DefinitionPath; no derivation edge, no parameter.

DefinitionPath leaves root draco, where `hostRoots` — literally the Java classpath — and its URLs and
streams were a leak against neutrality. On the carrier side they are the content. TypeLoader, still in
root, names it by its new package.

DEFINITIONPATH.SOURCE, DRAKE-FIRST, AND THE MISTAKE THAT PRECEDED IT

Increment 1 of the case body element was to free the `case` token by converting the two pinned
definitions carrying it inside opaque text. Only this one can go now — TypeName.equals becomes a
case-branch and needs the Case kind to exist first.

The first version of this conversion was WRONG under the grounding. It rewrote the match as a `loc`
plus an `if` tree, authored directly in JSON, which Drake cannot parse back (#61). The surface-loss
headline rose 15 -> 16 and the draft record called the rise "the corpus improving." It was the
opposite: authoring in a carrier what Drake cannot say makes the carrier MORE normative. The rule that
falls out — never author in JSON what Drake cannot parse — has 15 standing violations; this would have
been a sixteenth.

Redone the other way round. The .drake was authored:

    loc found [URL] sources parameters par resourcePath
    mon require parameters
      par found.size <= 1
      par s"draco: $resourcePath is defined at ${found.size} roots, …" + found.mkString(", ")
    = found.headOption

Every value is on the application surface Drake parses today. The .json is `DrakeCLI parse` of that;
the .scala is `GeneratorCLI generate` of the .json; `emit(parse(drake)) == drake` with zero drift. The
projection calls `sources` — which probes every root by opening a stream — exactly once, as before;
`require` guards the many-roots error; `headOption` covers empty and one. The loss count does not
move. That order — surface first, carrier derived — is what "Drake is the Source" means operationally.

SPEC, AND WHAT IS NOT DONE

drake.dlt: CASE-BRANCH is admitted in a dyn-with-body as well as an actor's `message` (Dev) — where
the projection domains' 44 dispatch sites live and where TypeName.equals will land. DYN-WITH-BODY's
statement list gains `*~case`. DIVERGENCES corrects 94b35cc's text: TypeName.equals converts, with a
default branch; it is not dropped.

Generator(L) still holds one function element, not rules; the six-way dispatch in the hand-written
Generator IS those rules, unwritten. Drake still carries `generator = Drake.emit`, a Target-shaped
face on a Source — whether Drake is also a Target is open. DRACO.md still calls X.json "normative";
under the grounding its honest name is the BOOTSTRAP CARRIER, the one the loader can read before
anything parses, and the surface-loss baseline is the distance between Drake-as-Source in principle
and in fact. That line changes when the loader can read Drake.

